WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.632 --> 00:00:12.741
The passed rapidly across the state of Iowa.

2
00:00:14.162 --> 00:00:17.284
During the night it crossed the Mississippi River and entered Illinois.

3
00:00:18.665 --> 00:00:21.787
At four o'clock the next day, the travelers reached Chicago.

4
00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:27.772
Nine hundred miles separated Chicago from New York, but trains were plentiful along this route.

5
00:00:29.512 --> 00:00:36.257
Mr. Fogg led his companions from one train to another without pause as they traversed Indiana, Ohio, Pennsylvania,

6
00:00:36.457 --> 00:00:37.658
and New Jersey in a flash.

7
00:00:38.478 --> 00:00:41.140
Their last train followed the Hudson River into New York.

8
00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:46.624
Late on the evening of December 11, it stopped at the station, right beside the pier where the

9
00:00:46.764 --> 00:00:48.025
ocean-going steamers docked.

10
00:00:49.366 --> 00:00:53.188
But the steamer for Liverpool had left port three-quarters of an hour before!

11
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:55.710
Passepartout was crushed.

12
00:00:56.431 --> 00:01:00.169
Mr. Fogg, however, merely said, "We will decide what is best tomorrow."

13
00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.130
They crossed the Hudson River on a ferryboat and took a carriage to a hotel on Broadway.

14
00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:12.472
The next morning, after instructing Passepartout and Aouda to be ready at a moment's notice, Mr.

15
00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:13.492
Fogg left the hotel.

16
00:01:14.913 --> 00:01:15.793
It was 7:00 a.m.

17
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:16.733
on December 12.

18
00:01:17.513 --> 00:01:18.934
Until 8:45 p.m.

19
00:01:18.974 --> 00:01:20.514
on December 21—Mr.

20
00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:25.016
Fogg's last minute for completing his journey—there remained 9 days, 13 hours, and 45 minutes.

21
00:01:27.076 --> 00:01:30.377
Mr. Fogg went to the banks of the Hudson to scout among the anchored ships.

22
00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:34.698
They were mostly sailing boats, which would be too slow.

23
00:01:35.698 --> 00:01:39.680
He was ready to give up hope when he spotted a trading vessel, its stack puffing a cloud

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.040
of smoke, ready to depart.

25
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.681
He called out for the captain, who presented himself immediately.

26
00:01:46.161 --> 00:01:50.703
He was a man around fifty, a sort of sea-wolf with big eyes and a growling voice.

27
00:01:51.523 --> 00:01:52.923
His name was Andrew Speedy.

28
00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:55.664
"Is your ship a fast one?"

29
00:01:55.996 --> 00:01:56.516
asked Mr.

30
00:01:56.556 --> 00:01:57.837
Fogg. "Yes.

31
00:01:58.377 --> 00:02:02.038
Its name is the Henrietta, and it is well known," said the captain.

32
00:02:02.058 --> 00:02:05.899
"Will you carry me and three other passengers to Liverpool?"

33
00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:06.739
asked Mr.

34
00:02:06.779 --> 00:02:08.279
Fogg. "No.

35
00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:11.760
I am setting out for Bordeaux, France," replied the captain.

36
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.661
"I am willing to pay any sum of money," said Mr.

37
00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:19.482
Fogg. "It makes no difference," said Captain Speedy.

38
00:02:19.502 --> 00:02:22.183
"My destination is Bordeaux."

39
00:02:22.203 --> 00:02:24.904
"I will buy your ship," insisted Mr.

40
00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:28.009
Fogg. "No," refused the captain flatly.

41
00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:32.890
Phileas Fogg did not betray his disappointment, but the situation was serious.

42
00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.451
Up to this point, money had removed all difficulties.

43
00:02:37.511 --> 00:02:38.231
He thought hard.

44
00:02:38.971 --> 00:02:41.072
He must cross the Atlantic one way or another.

45
00:02:41.092 --> 00:02:43.813
"Well, will you carry me to Bordeaux?"

46
00:02:44.153 --> 00:02:44.633
he asked.

47
00:02:44.653 --> 00:02:47.254
"No," answered Captain Speedy.

48
00:02:47.274 --> 00:02:50.675
"I offer you two thousand dollars," said Fogg.

49
00:02:50.695 --> 00:02:52.455
"Per passenger?"

50
00:02:52.835 --> 00:02:54.596
asked the captain, becoming interested.

51
00:02:54.616 --> 00:02:58.774
"Yes. Two thousand dollars per passenger," agreed Mr.

52
00:02:58.814 --> 00:03:01.777
Fogg. Captain Speedy scratched his head.

53
00:03:02.437 --> 00:03:05.199
He had eight thousand dollars to gain without changing his route!

54
00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:08.222
"I start in an hour," he said simply.

55
00:03:08.242 --> 00:03:11.264
"We will be onboard," said Mr.

56
00:03:11.304 --> 00:03:15.147
Fogg, and true to his word, he, Aouda, Passepartout, and Mr.

57
00:03:15.187 --> 00:03:18.370
Fix were standing on the deck when the Henrietta lifted its anchor.

58
00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:25.776
The next day as the ship steamed eastward, a man mounted the bridge to check the ship's position.

59
00:03:26.836 --> 00:03:29.937
He unrolled the navigation charts and gazed out over the sea.

60
00:03:31.058 --> 00:03:32.619
Who was in command of the Henrietta?

61
00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:35.060
It was none other than Phileas Fogg!

62
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.040
What had happened was simple.

63
00:03:37.761 --> 00:03:39.581
Phileas Fogg wanted to go to Liverpool.

64
00:03:40.342 --> 00:03:41.822
Captain Speedy would not take him.

65
00:03:42.683 --> 00:03:43.143
So Mr.

66
00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:46.825
Fogg had bribed all the sailors, and they had switched their loyalty to him.

67
00:03:47.825 --> 00:03:50.686
Captain Speedy was locked in his cabin screaming loudly.

68
00:03:51.867 --> 00:03:53.047
Passepartout thought Mr.

69
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.828
Fogg's maneuver was simply glorious.

70
00:03:55.732 --> 00:03:57.333
It was clear, as he watched Mr.

71
00:03:57.353 --> 00:03:59.254
Fogg manage the craft, that Mr.

72
00:03:59.274 --> 00:04:00.215
Fogg had been a sailor.

73
00:04:01.116 --> 00:04:05.739
Therefore if the sea did not become too stormy, if the wind did not turn against them, if

74
00:04:05.859 --> 00:04:11.423
no accident happened to the boat or its machinery, the Henrietta would arrive in Liverpool in nine days,

75
00:04:11.743 --> 00:04:12.904
on December 21.

76
00:04:14.324 --> 00:04:16.126
The first days of the crossing went smoothly.

77
00:04:17.106 --> 00:04:22.450
They passed the banks of Newfoundland without encountering fog or heavy seas, but on the third day, the

78
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.292
clouds darkened and the wind blew from the southeast.

79
00:04:26.208 --> 00:04:27.728
To maintain his speed, Mr.

80
00:04:27.748 --> 00:04:31.069
Fogg instructed the crew to increase the fires in the engine's boilers.

81
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.191
On December 16 the engineer, looking serious, came on deck and spoke with Mr.

82
00:04:37.231 --> 00:04:39.952
Fogg. "You are certain of what you tell me?"

83
00:04:40.332 --> 00:04:40.892
asked Mr.

84
00:04:40.912 --> 00:04:43.813
Fogg. "Absolutely," said the engineer.

85
00:04:44.573 --> 00:04:47.254
The engine's fires had been burning hot for too long.

86
00:04:47.974 --> 00:04:49.014
They were running out of coal.

87
00:04:49.915 --> 00:04:51.255
Mr. Fogg did not hesitate.

88
00:04:51.275 --> 00:04:55.036
"Feed the fires until the coal is exhausted," he said.

89
00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.683
The vessel continued at full speed, but three days away from Liverpool, the engineer announced that the coal

90
00:05:01.743 --> 00:05:02.264
was gone.

91
00:05:03.184 --> 00:05:06.306
Mr. Fogg asked Passepartout to bring Captain Speedy on deck.

92
00:05:06.986 --> 00:05:08.447
The captain appeared in a rage.

93
00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:11.949
"I have sent for you, sir—" began Mr.

94
00:05:11.989 --> 00:05:13.530
Fogg. "Pirate!"

95
00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:14.870
yelled Captain Speedy.

96
00:05:15.751 --> 00:05:18.592
Fogg continued, "—to ask you to sell me your ship."

97
00:05:18.612 --> 00:05:20.754
"By all the devils, no!"

98
00:05:21.174 --> 00:05:21.934
yelled the captain.

99
00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:25.580
"But I shall be obliged to burn her," said Mr.

100
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.363
Fogg. Mr.

101
00:05:30.403 --> 00:05:34.446
Fogg managed to find us a boat to Liverpool, though I didn't expect him to become the captain!

102
00:05:35.346 --> 00:05:39.969
Luckily, there were many boats traveling along the Hudson, one of the busiest rivers in the United States.

103
00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:44.672
The Hudson runs through New York State in the eastern part of the United States.

104
00:05:45.973 --> 00:05:50.496
It is 315 miles long and forms part of the boundary between the states of New York and

105
00:05:50.536 --> 00:05:51.056
New Jersey.

106
00:05:51.744 --> 00:05:56.848
The river is named after Henry Hudson, an Englishman who was sailing for the Dutch East India Company

107
00:05:57.088 --> 00:05:59.229
and explored it around 1609.

108
00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.273
Parts of the river are so beautiful that many artists have been inspired to paint it and the

109
00:06:04.333 --> 00:06:05.314
surrounding landscape.

110
00:06:06.214 --> 00:06:09.637
This art movement even has a name: the Hudson River School.

111
00:06:10.317 --> 00:06:13.239
As we rushed past, I would have liked to stop to paint the river too.

112
00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.801
But as you know, Mr.

113
00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:18.323
Fogg, was in quite a hurry to reach another body of water—the Atlantic Ocean!
